*This project has been created as part of the 42 curriculum by akmarjou.*

# ft_printf

## Description

**ft_printf** is a re-implementation of the C standard library function `printf`, packaged as a static library (`libftprintf.a`). The goal is to learn how variadic functions work (`<stdarg.h>`), how to parse a format string, and how to convert values (integers, unsigned integers, hexadecimal, pointers) to text without relying on the standard library formatting functions.

### Supported conversions

| Specifier | Description                                        |
|-----------|----------------------------------------------------|
| `%c`      | A single character                                 |
| `%s`      | A string (prints `(null)` if the pointer is NULL)  |
| `%p`      | A pointer in hexadecimal (prints `(nil)` if NULL)  |
| `%d`/`%i` | A signed decimal integer                           |
| `%u`      | An unsigned decimal integer                        |
| `%x`      | An unsigned integer in lowercase hexadecimal       |
| `%X`      | An unsigned integer in uppercase hexadecimal       |
| `%%`      | A literal percent sign                             |

`ft_printf` returns the number of characters printed, or `-1` if a `write` call fails.

### Project structure

| File                | Role                                                              |
|---------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `ft_printf.c`       | Main function: loops over the format string, calls the handler    |
| `ft_format_handel.c`| Dispatches each specifier to the right conversion routine         |
| `ft_base.c`         | Hexadecimal output for pointers (`ft_base`) and `%x`/`%X` (`ft_base_x`) |
| `ft_itoa.c`         | Converts a signed `int` to a string                               |
| `ft_itoaf.c`        | Converts an `unsigned int` to a string                            |
| `ft_putchar.c`, `ft_putstr.c`, `ft_strlen.c` | Basic output / helper functions          |
| `ft_printf.h`       | Header with prototypes and includes                               |
| `Makefile`          | Builds the library                                                |

## Instructions

### Compilation

```bash
make        # builds libftprintf.a
make clean  # removes object files
make fclean # removes object files and the library
make re     # fclean + all
```

The code is compiled with `cc -Wall -Wextra -Werror`.

### Usage

Include the header in your code and link against the library:

```c
#include "ft_printf.h"

int main(void)
{
	int n;

	n = ft_printf("Hello %s! %d + %d = %i | hex: %x %X | ptr: %p | %%\n",
			"42", 1, 2, 3, 255, 255, &n);
	ft_printf("printed %d characters\n", n);
	return (0);
}
```

```bash
cc -Wall -Wextra -Werror main.c libftprintf.a -o test
./test
```

## Algorithm and data structures

### Overview of the flow

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["ft_printf<br/>va_start + loop on format"] --> B["Normal character<br/>ft_putchar"]
    A --> C["Found percent sign<br/>ft_format_handel"]
    C --> D["c, s<br/>ft_strchrstr"]
    C --> E["d, i, p<br/>ft_nmbr_address"]
    C --> F["u, x, X<br/>ft_nmbr_lowupp"]
    D --> G["putchar, putstr<br/>write(1, ...)"]
    E --> H["itoa, base<br/>int to text, pointer"]
    F --> I["itoaf, base_x<br/>unsigned to text, hex"]
```

### Format string parsing

`ft_printf` walks through the format string one character at a time with a single index `i`:

- If the character is `%` and is followed by another character, the next character (the specifier) is passed to `ft_format_handel`, and the index is advanced past it.
- Otherwise, the character is printed directly with `ft_putchar`.

A counter accumulates the number of characters printed. After every output operation, the return value is checked: if it is `-1` (a failed `write`), `va_end` is called and `-1` is returned immediately.

### Variadic arguments

The `va_list` is created once in `ft_printf` and passed **by pointer** (`va_list *`) down to the handlers. This is important: the argument cursor must advance in the caller as well, so each `va_arg` call consumes the next argument for the following specifier. The specifiers are grouped into three small static functions (`ft_strchrstr`, `ft_nmbr_address`, `ft_nmbr_lowupp`) to respect the 42 Norm (max 25 lines per function), and `ft_format_handel` simply dispatches to the right group.

### Number to string conversion (`%d`, `%i`, `%u`)

- **Counting digits:** a first function computes how many characters the result needs (digits, plus one for the sign if negative, and one for `0`).
- **Allocation:** `malloc(count + 1)` creates the exact-size buffer. A `char *` buffer was chosen because the string is then printed in a single `write` call, and its length gives the return count for free.
- **Filling from the end:** digits are extracted with `n % 10` and `n / 10` and written from the last position backwards, so no reversal is needed.
- **Edge cases:** `0` is handled separately, and `INT_MIN` is handled specially because `-INT_MIN` overflows an `int`. Its first digits are written manually and the rest is processed as a normal positive number.

### Hexadecimal conversion (`%x`, `%X`, `%p`)

A recursive approach is used. For a value `p`:

1. If `p >= 16`, recurse with `p / 16` (this prints the more significant digits first).
2. Then print `base[p % 16]`, where `base` is `"0123456789abcdef"` (or the uppercase version for `%X`).

Recursion naturally produces the digits in the correct order without a buffer, so no allocation is needed. For `%p`, the pointer is cast to `unsigned long` (large enough to hold an address on 64-bit systems), `0x` is printed first, and a NULL pointer prints `(nil)`.

